tests.numbers:
Do Nothing spends no new programme cash: no equity, no borrowing, no change in leverage. The board shows debt of $40B on EV of $110B; on the record the company has net cash of £2.1bn (July 2026) and single-A ratings.
I confirm the capital frame:
1. cash from the Trent and Joint Venture fleets goes to the balance sheet first;
2. then the 30-40% dividend;
3. then investment and further distributions on fit, value and timing, through the Investment Committee at mid-to-high-teens hurdles.
No balance-sheet trigger is in play. A fall in the brief's ΔPV is not a trigger.

Do Nothing is the PV-best row in every column, so the premium is $0. If the CEO wanted either item, the premium would be:
- upgrade: about $6.48B, more than double the $3B cap;
- UltraFan WB: about $8.58-8.88B, against the $1B sub-cap.

The plan has been public since round 1 and live since the NGSA selection, before the 2037 EIS:
- a partner-shop network on the Singapore Joint Venture model;
- repair technology qualified in those shops;
- a spare-engine pool;
- an early-years intervention team.
The board reports no gate status, and nothing on the record shows a slip.

Under P&W's terms each partner supports its own modules. Our plan covers ours and stays ours, live before any Rolls-Royce block enters service. P&W's shops add capacity only after that, never in place of our plan.

Scale: 3,744 RR-share engines delivered by 2045 and 8,124 by 2050, so the first shop-visit wave builds through the late 2040s, and partner shops scale with it.

Second airframe: the plan is sized before any fps EIS. Internal gate only: 2052 on a 7-year launch this round, 2055 on a 10-year or Embraer launch. These dates never go public.
